+### Manuscript history: the provenance repo
+
+The workbench's own "Manuscript history" panel and the diff form beside it
+read a second thing the run keeps beside its manuscript:
+`workspace/<slug>/provenance.git`, a real git repository, one per run. It is
+**bare** — there is no working tree and nothing ever checked out of it — so
+any git tool can point at it directly (`git --git-dir
+workspace/<slug>/provenance.git log --all`, `gitk`, a GUI) without ever
+touching a file inside the run itself. ScieFlow never checks it out either:
+every commit is built with plumbing (`hash-object`, `mktree`, `commit-tree`,
+`update-ref`), never `git add` or `git checkout`, so there is no branch to
+switch into and no working tree an agent's sandboxed dispatch could leave
+dirty or write into. A `.git` living inside `manuscript/` would be exactly
+that kind of thing to corrupt; a bare repo beside it, built only through
+plumbing, gives an agent no reason to ever touch it.
+
+**The layout follows one rule: `main` carries what the run produced
+collectively, and `draft/<agent>` carries what that agent produced alone.**
+Round-structured material gets its own numbered directory; material that is
+written once sits at the branch root.
+
+```
+main (branch)
+  outline.md                 the paper's outline, once written
+  merge_1/                   the workbench's first merge round
+    sections/<section>.tex     that round's merged sections
+    curation.yml                the curation as it stood for that round
+  merge_2/ …                 later merge rounds, same shape
+  review_1/                  paper-review's first draft round
+    review.md, response.md
+  review_2/ …
+
+draft/<agent> (branch)        one branch per agent the run actually used
+  findings.json, gaps.json
+  sections/<section>.tex     that agent's own draft, never a merged one
+  reviews/<agent>-on-<other>.json
+  review_1/                  paper-review's first draft round, this agent's side
+    <agent>-on-<other>.json
+    response-<agent>.md
+  review_2/ …
+```
+
+`curation.yml` is worth one caveat: it sits under whichever merge round was
+most recent *when a sync ran*, not under every round. There is only one live
+`curation/document.yml` on disk, so once a run moves on to round 2, round 1's
+directory keeps its sections forever but stops gaining a fresh `curation.yml`
+of its own — the round 1 copy still exists in the commit made while round 1
+was current, just not at the current tip's `merge_1/` path. `curation.yml` is
+kept at all because "which passages were kept, from whom, at that round" is
+the provenance most worth having, and otherwise it exists only in that one
+live file.
+
+`merge_N/` and `review_N/` are deliberately two different counters, not one
+shared `round_N/`: a merge round comes from the workbench's own curation
+rounds, a review round from `paper-review`'s draft-review cycle, and the two
+advance independently. Naming them apart is what makes `main:merge_2` and
+`main:review_2` two unrelated things rather than a collision waiting to
+happen the day the counts diverge — which they will, since nothing keeps
+them in step.
+
+**Branch names are never configured — they come from the agents a run
+actually used**, discovered from its own artifacts: the subdirectories of
+`manuscript/drafts/`, the stems of `findings/<agent>.json` and
+`gaps/<agent>.json`, and the `<reviewer>-on-<author>` pairs in `reviews/` and
+`review/draft-round-<n>/`. Nothing here reads `config/agents.yml` — an
+agent's presence in the run's own files is evidence it acted, which is a
+different question from which agents are merely configured. Adding an agent
+to that config therefore needs no change here, and a run that used only one
+agent gets exactly one `draft/` branch. A discovered name that cannot safely
+become part of a git ref (git's own rules, plus the same path-safety checks
+the drafts directory itself already applies) is skipped for that agent only,
+and reported — it never fails the rest of the sync.
+
+**Three diffs cover what a person actually wants to compare**, and each is
+one `git diff` against two points in this repo — you can run them yourself
+against `provenance.git`, or read them from the workbench's diff form, which
+runs the same comparison:
+
+```
+git diff main:merge_1 main:merge_2
+```
+What the merging agent changed between two rounds.
+
+```
+git diff draft/claude:sections main:merge_1/sections
+```
+How the merge round altered claude's own proposal.
+
+```
+git diff draft/claude:review_1 draft/claude:review_2
+```
+How claude's reviewing changed between two review rounds.
+
+The second one needs `:sections` on *both* sides, and this is the part worth
+getting right rather than guessing at: `draft/claude:sections` is a
+directory containing that agent's `.tex` files directly, while
+`main:merge_1` is a round directory that itself contains a `sections/`
+subdirectory (plus a `curation.yml`) — one level deeper. Diff the two at
+those mismatched depths and `git diff` doesn't fail or warn; it just finds no
+path in common between the two trees and reports it as it would report any
+two unrelated directories: every file under `draft/claude:sections` shown as
+deleted, every file under `main:merge_1` shown as newly added, with no
+line-level comparison ever happening. Pointing both sides at `sections/`
+puts them at the same depth, so git matches `results.tex` against
+`results.tex` and produces the line-level diff you actually came for. The
+workbench's own diff form never lets you make this mistake — its point
+picker only ever offers points at compatible depths — but the same
+mismatch is there to walk into if you build the `git diff` command by hand.
+
+**The repo is derived, never authoritative.** Everything in it is rebuilt
+from the workspace's own files — `findings/`, `gaps/`, `manuscript/drafts/`,
+`manuscript/curation/`, `review/`, `outline/` — which remain the actual
+source of truth. Deleting `provenance.git` costs history and nothing else:
+the next page load rebuilds whatever the workspace can still support, from
+scratch if need be. This is exactly why a failed sync never fails a merge
+round — a round that dispatched an agent and spent budget already happened,
+and losing its provenance commit is a strictly smaller loss than pretending
+the round itself failed over a git error. The sync itself runs after every
+successful merge round and again on every load of the workbench page, so a
+missed or delayed sync is caught the next time either happens; because a
+round's output is preserved as a *path* (`merge_2/`) rather than only as a
+commit boundary, even two rounds landing in the same commit still leave both
+`merge_1/` and `merge_2/` addressable and diffable on their own.
+
+**It is local only.** Nothing here is ever pushed, fetched or shared —
+no remote is configured, and there is no Overleaf or GitHub integration to
+speak of. This repo answers "what changed, and whose was it" for someone
+already looking at the run; DVC (see [DVC storage](DVC_STORAGE.md)) still
+carries the bytes, and provenance does not replace it.
+
+**`git` is an optional dependency.** If it isn't on the host's `PATH`, the
+panel says so in plain words instead of showing a history, and nothing else
+on the workbench is affected — drafting, curation, merging and previews all
+work exactly as they do when git is present.
+
 Every form on these pages posts back to the same page
 (`303 See Other` on success, so a reload never repeats the action) or
 carries you to `?error=<message>` on refusal — the same
 `service.ServiceError` message the CLI would print. There is no separate
 success/failure JSON to keep in sync with the terminal's exit codes. The
 Start page is the one exception: it still redirects on success, but a
 refusal re-renders the form directly rather than round-tripping through
 `?error=...` (see [Pages](#pages) above).
 
